B. Flip the Bit (Easy Version)
Time limit: 1.5 seconds
Memory limit: 256 megabytes

This is the easy version of the problem. The difference between the versions is that in this version, there is exactly one special index (k = 1).

You are given a binary array a of length n and k special indices p1, p2, ..., pk (1 <= pi <= n). It is given that the values ai of all elements at special indices are the same.

In one operation, you can choose a range [l, r] (1 <= l <= r <= n) such that the range contains at least one special index (l <= pi <= r) and flip all bits aj for l <= j <= r. Flipping a bit changes 0 to 1 and 1 to 0.

Let x denote the value at special indices before any operations are applied. Find the minimum number of operations required to make all elements of the array equal to x.

Input:
The first line contains the number of test cases t (1 <= t <= 10000).
The first line of each test case contains two integers n and k (1 <= n <= 200000; k = 1) - the length of the array and the number of special indices.
The second line contains n integers a1, a2, ..., an (0 <= ai <= 1) - the elements of the array.
The third line contains k integers p1, p2, ..., pk (1 <= p1 < p2 < ... < pk <= n) - the special indices.

Output:
For each test case, output a single integer - the minimum number of operations required.

Strategy:
This one was much harder than i thought a B should be. Well, the key is realizing that the invariant is that we will need to do the number of operations that there are different clouds of the same value but with a catch. All the operations need to include the special index. So we have the special index at some position i and there's a left and right subarray we need to make into a uniform cloud with the same number as at i. The immediate conclusion from this is that since we are just switching 1s and 0s we can do both subarrays simultaneously therefore the number of operations will be max(leftOp, rightOp). Now the number of operations for the subarray is pretty simple. Since all the operations need to be anchored at i we will just slowly be moving the pointer at the boundary and for all values with a different values than i we need to switch them but that will affect the values same as i immediately next to it and we need to switch them back before we move onto the next non-i cloud. Therefore the number of operations needs to be the numbers of different switches from one cloud to another. We count these for both subarrays. Also, since all the operations have the i value and it needs to be the same at the end the number of operations needs to be even. Therefore, if it is not at the end we do one more just on i to fix it. We output max(leftOp, rightOp) adjusted for being even.